---
title: Your first project
description: Install LUI, create a native window and button, check your code and run your first app.
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

Make a native window with a button that changes its text. By the end, you will know the everyday **edit → check → run** loop.

## 1. Get your tools ready

You need a **Windows 64-bit PC** and **VS Code**. This tutorial uses SDK **0.1.0-preview.1**. It is an early, unsigned preview; first-time setup on a completely fresh Windows PC is still being tested.

1. Open the [LUI preview release](https://github.com/gmoddev/LUI/releases/tag/sdk-v0.1.0-preview.1) and download `LUI-SDK-Windows-x64-0.1.0-preview.1.zip`.
2. Extract the whole ZIP to a folder you want to keep. Keep every subfolder inside `SDK-Preview` together.
3. In VS Code, open the Command Palette with **Ctrl+Shift+P**. Run **Extensions: Install from VSIX** and select `SDK-Preview/Editor/LUI.vsix`.
4. Run **LUI: Choose SDK** and select the `SDK-Preview` folder containing `SdkManifest.json`.

Windows may display a warning because the preview is unsigned. Only run a download whose source you trust. If setup fails, read the **LUI Preview** output in VS Code and see [setup help](../../guides/setup/).

**You should see:** the LUI commands in VS Code. Application authors do not need the .NET SDK or a C++ compiler to use this bundle.

## 2. Create your project

Run **LUI: Create Project**, select an existing parent folder and enter `HelloLui` as the project name. Choose a new name if that folder already exists. The extension needs a trusted workspace before creating or running projects; only trust code you understand and trust.

VS Code opens the new folder. Its important files are:

```text
HelloLui/
  Src/Main.luau
  lui.json
  LuiToolchain.json
```

- `Src/Main.luau` is your app's entry file.
- `lui.json` describes its sources and settings.
- `LuiToolchain.json` remembers the exact SDK version and inventory. Keep this file with your project.

